// pbread — one message, read field by field, with no schema and no
// buffer the caller did not give.
//
// Every body is a `todo()`.  The signatures are the design, and the
// compiler checks them as claims; see the README for the stamp.
//
// THE SHAPE IS FEED AND DRAIN.  The host pumps chunks in and takes
// finished fields out (`docs/publishing.md` § How a `core` package
// takes bytes from its host).  That is right here because a protobuf
// field is small and a message is read strictly left to right — there
// is no index, no back-reference and nothing to seek to.
//
// A MESSAGE IS NOT SELF-DELIMITING, which is the fact that shapes this
// whole module.  There is no header, no length and no end marker: a
// message ends where its bytes end, and something outside the format
// has to say where that is.  gRPC says it with a five-byte frame,
// a file says it with its own length prefix, a field says it with the
// length-delimited wire type.  So this reader reads UNTIL THE HOST SAYS
// STOP — `finish` is that statement — and `feed` never announces a
// message boundary it cannot see.
//
// WHY THE EVENTS CARRY NO FIELD NAMES.  This module has no schema.  A
// length-delimited field is a string, a byte string, a submessage or a
// packed repeated field, and only a `.proto` file knows which; a varint
// is any of eight declared types.  So the events carry the wire shape
// and the raw bytes, and `pbdyn` is the module that puts a descriptor
// over them.  A caller with a generated struct does the same thing with
// generated code.
//
// WHAT IT REFUSES, AND WHAT IT KEEPS.  It refuses what is not protobuf
// at all — wire codes 6 and 7, a zero field number, a varint past ten
// bytes, a length past the end, nesting past the limit.  It keeps
// everything else, including field numbers in the descriptor
// language's reserved range: a peer's mistake is not a reason to drop a
// connection, and `pbwrite` is where writing one is refused.

use std.bytes
use pbunknown

// What a reader will not do, whatever it is asked.
//
// Both numbers are defences against the sender rather than against the
// format: a length-delimited field is an allocation instruction from
// whoever sent it, and protobuf's nesting is unbounded by
// specification.  Every implementation bounds both; this one says where
// the numbers are instead of hiding them.
pub struct PbLimits
    // How deep groups and submessages may nest.  100 by default, which
    // is what the C++ implementation has used since 2008.
    max_depth: Int
    // The most bytes one message may occupy.  A field that announces
    // more is `PbMessageTooLarge` before anything is reserved for it.
    max_message: Int
    // Whether a `string` field's bytes are checked for UTF-8 as they
    // are read.  On by default: proto3 requires it, and a `Str` whose
    // bytes are not UTF-8 has a length nobody agrees on.  A reader
    // walking a message it has no schema for does not know which
    // length-delimited fields are strings, so this applies where
    // `pbdyn` has a descriptor and nowhere else.
    check_utf8: Bool

// The limits a server should start from: depth 100, a 64 MiB message,
// UTF-8 checked.
//
// ```novo
// use pbread
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${pbread.default_limits().max_depth}")   // 100
// ```
pub fn default_limits() -> PbLimits []
    todo("pbread.default_limits")

// The limits for a program reading messages from somewhere it does not
// trust: depth 32, a 1 MiB message, UTF-8 checked.
//
// Named rather than left to a caller to assemble, because the numbers
// that matter are the ones somebody chose on purpose.
pub fn strict_limits() -> PbLimits []
    todo("pbread.strict_limits")

// One field's payload, in the shape its key announced and no other.
//
// This is deliberately NOT a typed value.  A varint is `int32`,
// `int64`, `uint32`, `uint64`, `sint32`, `sint64`, `bool` or an enum
// depending on a `.proto` file this module has not read, and answering
// one of the eight would be a guess.  `pbdyn.value_of` is where a
// descriptor turns one of these into a typed value.
pub enum PbRaw
    // Wire type 0.  The varint's sixty-four-bit pattern, unfolded by
    // nothing.
    PbRawVarint(bits: Int)
    // Wire type 1.  Eight little-endian bytes as a sixty-four-bit
    // pattern.
    PbRawFixed64(bits: Int)
    // Wire type 2.  The payload, key and length prefix excluded.
    PbRawBytes(data: Bytes)
    // Wire type 5.  Four little-endian bytes as a thirty-two-bit
    // pattern.
    PbRawFixed32(bits: Int)

// What the reader drained.
//
// `PbGroupStart` and `PbGroupEnd` bracket a proto2 group's fields, so a
// caller sees the nesting rather than a flat run with a field number it
// has to match up itself.  A submessage produces ONE `PbFieldSeen` with
// a `PbRawBytes` payload — nesting it would mean knowing it is a
// submessage, and only a schema knows that.
pub enum PbEvent
    // A field arrived whole.  `raw_len` is what the field occupied
    // including its key, which is what `pbunknown.keep` needs and what
    // a caller computing an offset into the original buffer adds.
    PbFieldSeen(tag: PbTag, value: PbRaw, at: Int, raw_len: Int)
    // A group opened: wire type 3, with this field number.
    PbGroupStart(field: Int, at: Int)
    // A group closed: wire type 4, with the same number.
    PbGroupEnd(field: Int, at: Int)

// One turn of the reader: the reader after the step, what it produced,
// and what it is waiting for.
//
// `need` is a HINT and never a promise — a varint has no announced
// length, so the answer is 1 whenever the reader is inside one.  It is
// 0 when the reader is between fields and would be content to stop.
pub struct PbStep
    reader: PbReader
    events: [PbEvent]
    need: Int

// A message being read: the bytes not yet consumed, the group stack,
// and how far in it is.
//
// Opaque on purpose.  Everything a caller needs is an accessor below,
// and a field a caller reaches into is a field this package can never
// change.
pub struct PbReader
    limits: PbLimits
    pending: Bytes
    offset: Int
    depth: Int
    groups: [Int]

// A fresh reader over one message.
pub fn reader(limits: PbLimits) -> PbReader []
    todo("pbread.reader")

// Feed a chunk and take every field that finished.
//
// A field split across two chunks stays pending until the rest arrives;
// nothing is copied twice and nothing is parsed twice.  A chunk that
// finishes nothing is an empty event list and not an error — on a
// socket that is the ordinary case.
//
// ```novo
// use std.bytes
// use pbread
//
// fn main() [io]
//     // field 1, length-delimited, "abc".
//     let buf = bytes.from_hex("0a03616263") ?? bytes.zeros(0)
//     match pbread.feed(pbread.reader(pbread.default_limits()), bytes.to_byte_list(buf))
//         Ok(s)  => println("${s.events.len()}")   // 1
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn feed(r: PbReader, chunk: [u8]) -> Result<PbStep, PbDecodeError> []
    todo("pbread.feed")

// Say that the message ends here.
//
// The host is the only party that knows where a message stops, so this
// is how it says so.  Bytes still pending are `PbTruncated`; a group
// still open is `PbUnclosedGroup`.  A message that ends cleanly answers
// a step with `need` 0 and whatever the last chunk finished.
pub fn finish(r: PbReader) -> Result<PbStep, PbDecodeError> []
    todo("pbread.finish")

// How many bytes the reader has consumed since it was made.
//
// The number every `PbDecodeError`'s `at` is counted in, so a log line
// from this end and one from the other end name the same byte.
pub fn offset(r: PbReader) -> Int []
    todo("pbread.offset")

// How many fed bytes are still waiting for the rest of their field.
pub fn pending_len(r: PbReader) -> Int []
    todo("pbread.pending_len")

// How many groups are open.
pub fn depth(r: PbReader) -> Int []
    todo("pbread.depth")

// Whether the reader is between fields, and so would accept `finish`
// without refusing.
pub fn at_boundary(r: PbReader) -> Bool []
    todo("pbread.at_boundary")

// ── the whole-buffer forms ───────────────────────────────────────────

// Every field of a message the caller already holds entirely.
//
// The convenience over `feed` and `finish` for the common case: a
// message that arrived inside a gRPC frame, a length-delimited field,
// or a file.
//
// ```novo
// use std.bytes
// use pbread
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let buf = bytes.from_hex("089601") ?? bytes.zeros(0)
//     match pbread.walk(buf, pbread.default_limits())
//         Ok(evs) => println("${evs.len()}")   // 1 — field 1, varint 150
//         Err(e)  => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn walk(src: Bytes, limits: PbLimits) -> Result<[PbEvent], PbDecodeError> []
    todo("pbread.walk")

// Every field of the `len` bytes at `off`.
//
// What a caller re-entering a submessage calls: a `PbRawBytes` payload
// is a nested message's bytes, and this reads them without copying them
// out first.
pub fn walk_range(src: Bytes, off: Int, len: Int,
                  limits: PbLimits) -> Result<[PbEvent], PbDecodeError> []
    todo("pbread.walk_range")

// Every element of a packed repeated field, decoded as `t`.
//
// A packed field arrives as one `PbRawBytes`, and only the schema knows
// that its bytes are a hundred `sint32`s rather than a string.  This is
// the function `pbdyn` calls once it does.
pub fn unpack(data: Bytes, t: PbFieldType) -> Result<[PbRaw], PbDecodeError> []
    todo("pbread.unpack")

// ── keeping what is not known ────────────────────────────────────────

// Split a message's fields into the ones `wanted` names and the ones it
// does not.
//
// The second half is what a program must write back out unchanged, and
// this is the one call that makes doing so the default rather than an
// act of diligence.  `wanted` is the field numbers this program's
// schema declares.
//
// ```novo
// use std.bytes
// use pbread
// use pbunknown
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let buf = bytes.from_hex("089601") ?? bytes.zeros(0)
//     match pbread.partition(buf, [2], pbread.default_limits())
//         Ok(p)  => println("${pbunknown.count(p.unknown)}")   // 1
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn partition(src: Bytes, wanted: [Int],
                 limits: PbLimits) -> Result<PbPartition, PbDecodeError> []
    todo("pbread.partition")

// A message split into the fields a schema declared and the fields it
// did not.
pub struct PbPartition
    known: [PbEvent]
    unknown: PbUnknownSet

// ── the bound effect parameter ───────────────────────────────────────

// Read one whole message from the caller's source.
//
// The pump written once inside the package, charged whatever the
// caller's source costs: `[io]` for a socket, nothing for a buffer
// (`docs/publishing.md` § How a `core` package takes bytes from its
// host).  `limit` is how many bytes the message is — the host knows,
// because a protobuf message is not self-delimiting.
//
// It does not replace `feed` and `finish`: a caller that cannot give up
// control of its own loop still needs the state machine.
pub fn drain<S: Read[e]>(r: PbReader, src: S, limit: Int) -> Result<PbStep, PbDecodeError> [e]
    todo("pbread.drain")
